{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "reasoning", "text": "The 1.5% error in batchcost is causing a ~ The 1.5% error in batchcost also leads to a 2.7% error in Q, which fails to meet the required accuracy. I will check if batch-level OT regularization is too low, and explore a unified OT solver approach to cut the current computation workload in half.\n\nIt has been found that matched-reg GeomLoss lacks sufficient accuracy. The core issue is that batch cost normalization tightens the effective reg, making Sinkhorn-based results too sensitive to errors. An exact EMD solve for Q is a viable option, and a cleaner alternative is to skip POT Sinkhorn entirely and derive batch cost from GeomLoss potentials.\n\nI’ve decided to use GeomLoss’ canonical potentials as a starting point, run a small number of log-domain Sinkhorn iterations with adjusted regularization for refinement, and adjust parameter settings to align effective regularization with POT’s for batch cost accuracy. This approach is faster than a full POT solve and meets the precision requirements.\n\nThe previously planned dual GeomLoss approach is found to have limited efficiency. I’ve adjusted the batch cost calculation scheme to use a coarser Sinkhorn solver for cost acceleration, and will test the optimized single-solve scheme against real data. Additionally, preliminary analysis suggests a full Sinkhorn solve for the target matrix may be feasible.\n\nI’ve confirmed the 3000×2000 cost matrix only takes up 6M float64 storage, and a full Sinkhorn solve may be faster than the batching approach with less complexity. I’ll run a quick timing test to verify this.\n"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc \"cat > /tmp/test_full_sinkhorn.py << 'PYEOF'\n\\\"\\\"\\\"Test: how fast is a single full Sinkhorn on N=3000, M=2000?\\\"\\\"\\\"\nimport numpy as np\nimport time\nfrom scipy.spatial.distance import cdist\nfrom scipy.special import logsumexp\n\nnp.random.seed(42)\nN, M = 3000, 2000\nx = np.random.randn(N, 10).astype(np.float64)\nr = np.random.randn(M, 10).astype(np.float64)\n\nt0 = time.time()\nC = cdist(x, r, 'sqeuclidean') / 2.0\nprint(f\\\"cdist time: {time.time() - t0:.3f}s, C shape: {C.shape}, C.nbytes/1e6: {C.nbytes/1e6:.1f}MB\\\")\n\n# Log-domain Sinkhorn\nreg = 0.1\nC_max = C.max()\nC_scaled = C / C_max\nreg_scaled = reg\na = np.ones(N) / N\nb = np.ones(M) / M\nlog_a = np.log(a)\nlog_b = np.log(b)\nlog_u = np.zeros(N)\nlog_v = np.zeros(M)\n\nt0 = time.time()\nfor i in range(500):\n    log_v_prev = log_v\n    M_mat = -C_scaled / reg_scaled + log_v[None, :]\n    log_u = log_a - logsumexp(M_mat, axis=1)\n    M_mat = -C_scaled / reg_scaled + log_u[:, None]\n    log_v = log_b - logsumexp(M_mat, axis=0)\n    if i % 100 == 99:\n        err = np.abs(log_v - log_v_prev).max()\n        print(f\\\"  iter {i+1}: error = {err:.2e}\\\")\n        if err < 1e-10:\n            break\n\nelapsed = time.time() - t0\nprint(f\\\"500 iters time: {elapsed:.3f}s\\\")\nprint(f\\\"Per iter: {elapsed/500*1000:.2f}ms\\\")\n\nf = reg_scaled * log_u * C_max  # potentials (unscaled)\ng = reg_scaled * log_v * C_max\nlog_P = log_u[:, None] + log_v[None, :] - C_scaled / reg_scaled\nP = np.exp(log_P) * a[:, None]  # Wait - need correct convention\n# P = diag(u)  @  K  @  diag(v), where K = exp(-C/reg), u,v are scaling vectors\n# Row sum: u * (K  @  v) = a  => log_u + log(K @ v) = log_a\n# Our logsumexp gives: log(sum_j K_ij v_j) = log(sum_j exp(-C_ij/reg + log_v_j))\n# So log_u = log_a - logsumexp(-C/reg + log_v, axis=1)\n# Then P_ij = u_i * K_ij * v_j = exp(log_u_i - C_ij/reg + log_v_j)\nP = np.exp(log_u[:, None] + log_v[None, :] - C_scaled / reg_scaled)\nprint(f\\\"P row sums (first 3): {P.sum(1)[:3]}, target: {1/N}\\\")\nprint(f\\\"P col sums (first 3): {P.sum(0)[:3]}, target: {1/M}\\\")\n\n# Entropic cost\not_cost = float(np.sum(P * C))\nprint(f\\\"OT cost (primal): {ot_cost:.6f}\\\")\nPYEOF\npython /tmp/test_full_sinkhorn.py\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
